mod:for_binding_chain_seed;
// 2026-11-12 (landing 177d2b5, row 2: `fn1`'s seed). A `for` BINDING OVER AN ARRAY OR A SLICE HOLDS WHAT ITS SOURCE'S ELEMENTS HOLD, before the body stores anything
// into it. Its own chain is written at the `for` with the refs a read of it by its name carries, so the store's walk finds where a pointer the binding was never
// assigned points, and the growth after the store is refused (BORROW-015). One cell a face, its names suffixed with the cell's: each is accepted by row 1's commit
// (9a0c382), by main (169d6d4) and by 177d2b3's main, and reads freed memory there (236 at -O0 and -O2, 97 under the detector). Each face's local twin
// (`SQN:b = sa[0i64];`) is refused by every build, and each face's right control is in `for_binding_chain_seed_ok.npk`.
// DEF-604's `fn1` (nitpick-compiler_99's cell of 177d2b4's row 9; `for (SQ:b in sa) { (<-(b.q)).lp = hq; }`, the member holds a caller's pointer from the array).
// TEN CELLS OF ITS FRAME (nitpick-compiler_106's probes; management_38's ruling "Row 2's item 1 is ruled" of Thu 11-12 01:19 EST, item 2, for the first nine, and its
// "Addendum to row 2's item 1" of Thu 11-12 01:38 EST, item 1, for the tenth): `fa_arr` (a local array), `fa_fld` (an array in a struct's field), `fa_ptr` (pointer
// elements), `fa_nest` (two loops), `fa_met` (the store by a pointer-receiver method), `fa_two` (two elements, the store under an `if`), `fa_late` and `fa_latep`
// (the source is given its pointer AFTER the `for`, inside an outer loop), `fa_late3` and `fa_slpp0` (both below).
// THE ROW'S PRICES, FIVE RIGHT PROGRAMS REFUSED. Each runs 67 on every build before this row, and each is refused at parity with its local twin, which every build
// refuses. For the first two the reason is that the analysis doesn't count a loop's trips, and each one's control without the growth is accepted (`fa_late2c_ok`,
// `fa_slppc_ok`). For the other three "the reasons are flow, path and D-286's claim, and none is a trip count" (the 01:58 ruling, item 5, named below).
// `fa_late2` (2026-11-12; the 01:19 ruling, item 3): `fa_late3` with TWO trips of its outer loop, where `fa_late3` has three. Two trips never store behind `mp0`;
// the third trip is the fault `fa_late3`.
// `fa_slpp` (2026-11-12; the 01:38 addendum, item 1): a local array `[mp0]` whose one element a first `for`, over a slice parameter, overwrites (`la[0i64] = p;`)
// before a second `for` over the array stores through it. With one element in the slice the store never lands behind `mp0`; with the slice EMPTY it does, and that
// is the fault `fa_slpp0`.
// THREE MORE OF `fa_late2`'s CLASS (Ailp-team_110's cells, from its fresh attack on row 2, as it wrote them). Each holds `fn1`'s `for` with its store, and no fault.
// Their local twins are refused by every build, so the `_ok` file holds no cell of theirs.
// `rp_repoint_ok` (2026-11-12; management_38's ruling "Row 2's fresh attack is ruled" of Thu 11-12 01:58 EST, item 5; flow): the member is written back
// (`m0.lp = h0p;`) after the callee's store and before the view is taken.
// `rp_other_ok` (2026-11-12; the 01:58 ruling, item 5; path): the array has two elements, and the store runs only for the one with `z == 2i64`, which holds the
// other pointer, so `m0` is never written.
// `rp_before_ok` (2026-11-12; the 01:58 ruling, item 5; D-286's claim): the growth runs BEFORE the view is taken.
error:E5;
